The '''Fifth National Government of New Zealand''' was the [[List of New Zealand governments|government of New Zealand]] for three parliamentary terms from 19 November 2008 to 26 October 2017. [[John Key]] served as National Leader and prime minister until December 2016, after which [[Bill English]] assumed the premiership until the National Government's defeat following the October 2017 government-forming negotiations.

After the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 general election]] the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] and its allies were able to form a government, taking over from [[Helen Clark]]'s [[Fifth Labour Government of New Zealand|Fifth Labour Government]]. It was subsequently reformed after the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011 general election]] with a reduced number of seats, and after the [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014 general election]] with a reduced share of the party vote but the same number of seats. The Government had [[confidence and supply agreement]]s with the following parties: [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]], [[United Future]], and the [[Māori Party]] – which gave the Government a majority on major legislation. The National Party also signed a [[memorandum of understanding]] with the [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] after the 2008 election, but this lapsed in 2011 and was not renewed.

