===Economic===
The Government was elected in the context of the [[late 2000s recession]].
* The [[Fifth Labour Government]]'s [[Climate Change Response (Emissions Trading) Amendment Act 2008|Emissions Trading Scheme]] was delayed and the [[Emissions Trading Scheme Review Committee]] was set up to review the [[New Zealand Emissions Trading Scheme]]<ref name="etsreview">{{Cite report
|url=http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/PB/SC/Documents/Reports/f/4/f/49DBSCH_SCR4485_1-Review-of-the-Emissions-Trading-Scheme-and-related.htm
|title=Review of the Emissions Trading Scheme and related matters
|series=I.23A
|publisher=New Zealand Parliament
|date= 31 August 2009
|access-date= 30 September 2009
}}</ref> in accordance with the coalition agreement with the [[ACT New Zealand|ACT Party]].<ref>{{cite news
|last=Fleming|first=Grant|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=10543330
|title=Emissions trading scheme up for review under Act deal
|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]
|date=16 November 2008
|access-date=1 December 2010 }}</ref> In November 2009, an amended version of the New Zealand Emissions Trading Scheme was adopted.<ref name="smith251109">{{cite web |url=http://beehive.govt.nz/release/balanced+new+law+important+step+climate+change
|title=Balanced new law important step on climate change
|publisher=New Zealand Government Press Release
|author=Hon Dr Nick Smith
|author-link=Nick Smith (New Zealand politician)
|date=25 November 2009
|access-date=14 June 2010 }}</ref>
* Personal tax cuts, reducing taxes on all income; the top personal tax rate was lowered from 39% to 38% and then 33%.<ref>{{cite news
|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10562448
|title=Spend tax cut or give it to the needy: PM
|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]
|first=Claire
|last=Trevett
|date=19 March 2009
|access-date= 25 January 2010}}</ref>
* Abolished the [[Loss Attributing Qualifying Company]] (LAQC) tax structure, which had allowed individuals (mainly property investors) to reduce their individual income tax by off setting their LAQCs losses (the [[Look-through company]] structure replaced LAQCs, but without the tax benefits).
* Increased GST from 12.5% to 15% in October 2010.
* Increased the minimum wage from $12.00 per hour to $13.00 per hour in its first term, and to $14.25 in its second term.<ref>{{Cite news
|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=10622598&ref=rss
|title=Can you survive on the minimum wage in NZ?
|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]
|date= 27 January 2010
|access-date=28 January 2010}}</ref> This represents a nominal 3.1% average annual increase, significantly lower than the previous government's nominal 7.9% annual average increase.
* Suspended payments to the [[New Zealand Superannuation Fund]].<ref>[https://www.nzsuperfund.co.nz/nz-super-fund-explained-purpose-and-mandate/contributions-suspension "CONTRIBUTIONS SUSPENSION"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140915072858/https://www.nzsuperfund.co.nz/nz-super-fund-explained-purpose-and-mandate/contributions-suspension |date=15 September 2014 }}, nzsuperfund.co.nz/</ref>
* Introduced the nine-day working fortnight for businesses who were considering laying off staff.
* Capped the minimum employers' contributions to [[KiwiSaver]] at 2%, the amount was due to increase to 4% by 2011 and gave employees the option to contribute as little as 2% of their income to KiwiSaver where previously the smallest contribution amount was 4%. The minimum employee and minimum employer contributions were raised to 3% in April 2013.
*Significant investments in the rebuilding of Christchurch after the [[2011 Christchurch earthquake|Canterbury earthquakes]]<ref>{{Cite web|title=Budget 2016: Overview|url=http://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/budget-2016-overview|access-date=2021-12-11|website=The Beehive|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Budget 2013: At a Glance|url=http://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/budget-2013-glance|access-date=2021-12-11|website=The Beehive|language=en}}</ref>

===Constitution===
* Repealed the [[Electoral Finance Act 2007]]<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=10557314|title=Unpopular electoral finance law fades into night|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|first=Claire|last=Trevett|date=18 February 2009|access-date=25 January 2010}}</ref>
* Introduced the [[Governor-General Act 2010]], to reform the [[Governor-General of New Zealand|Governor-General]]'s salary and allowances.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/document/49HansD_20100720_00000696/governor-general-bill-first-reading |title=Governor-General Bill First Reading|publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]]|access-date=22 November 2021}}</ref>
* A [[2011 New Zealand voting method referendum|second referendum alongside the 2011 election]] on [[Electoral reform in New Zealand|the voting system]], and after the majority voted in the referendum to retain the existing [[mixed member proportional]] system, an independent review on the workings of the MMP system.
* A [[Constitution of New Zealand#Constitutional Review|Constitutional Review]] (as part of confidence and supply agreement with the [[Māori Party]]) starting in 2011.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://tvnz.co.nz/politics-news/monarchy-debate-off-topic-in-constitutional-review-3953730|title=Monarchy debate off-topic in constitutional review|publisher=[[TVNZ]]|access-date=14 March 2011|date=8 December 2010}}</ref>

===Social policy===

* Extended the paid parental leave scheme by four weeks<ref name=":0"/>
* Introduced the [[Employment Relations Amendment Act 2008]] (the "90-day working bill") in December 2008 which allowed employers with less than 20 staff to dismiss an employee within the first 90 days of employment for no particular reason.<ref name="NZ_Herald_10547721">{{cite web |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=10547721 |title=90-day bill passes first test under heavy fire |author=Trevett, Claire |date=12 December 2008 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=30 September 2011}}</ref> In 2010 the bill was extended to all employers.
* Allowed employees to cash in their fourth week of annual leave, employees can now take 3 weeks holiday and be paid for the fourth while still working.<ref name="NZ_Herald_10563030">{{cite web |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/small-business/news/article.cfm?c_id=85&objectid=10563030 |title=Govt to let employers buy back holidays |author=Gower, Patrick |date=22 March 2009 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=30 September 2011}}</ref> The fourth week of annual leave was introduced by the previous government.
* A lifetime limit on student loans was introduced: if a student has studied more than 7 EFTS within their lifetime the student can no longer take out any further loans. Students receiving [[New Zealand Superannuation Fund]] payments or Veterans Pension can no longer receive the Student Allowance at the same time. Students are now required to pass more than half of their studies each year to receive a Student Loan or Allowance the following year, previously this requirement only affected the Student Allowance.
* Reformed social security benefits by consolidating seven major benefits into three new ones.
*In 2015, introduced a $790 million of extra spending to reduce hardship among children in New Zealand's poorest families.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Budget 2015: At a Glance|url=http://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/budget-2015-glance|access-date=2021-12-11|website=The Beehive|language=en}}</ref>
*Invested in insulating New Zealand homes via the ''Warm Up New Zealand: Healthy Homes'' programme which targets low-income households.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Budget 2013: At a Glance|url=http://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/budget-2013-glance|access-date=2021-12-11|website=The Beehive|language=en}}</ref>

===Foreign affairs===
* Allowed a U.S. navy ship into New Zealand for the New Zealand Navy 75th anniversary without confirmation regarding nuclear weapons for the first time in 33 years<ref>{{Cite web |date=2016-07-19 |title=PM pleased US sending ship to NZ |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/united-states-ship-to-visit-nz-for-first-time-in-33-years/6DH7PD7QAOUXNTFGZULGS3KDBE/?c_id=1&objectid=11678267 |access-date=2024-09-06 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ}}</ref>
* Won a seat on the [[United Nations Security Council|UN Security Council]] in the 2014 election, a process that started in 2004.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2014-09-28 |title=NZ wins seat: 'Victory for small states' |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/nz-wins-seat-on-security-council-victory-for-the-small-states/2AC43UL3YEWCZRAUFRBIAOZ3B4/?c_id=1&objectid=11343853 |access-date=2024-09-06 |website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ}}</ref>

=== Defence ===

* In 2012, New Zealand and the U.S. signed the Washington Declaration, strengthening military cooperation and defence relations, for the first time in more than 30 years.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/7133939/Agreement-with-US-sees-NZ-as-de-facto-ally|title=Agreement with US sees NZ as 'de facto' ally|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=20 June 2012 |language=en|access-date=23 May 2018}}</ref> 
* Removal of the position of Minister of Disarmament and Arms Control.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PO1112/S00195/minister-for-disarmament-and-arms-control-disestablished.htm|title=Minister for Disarmament and Arms Control disestablished {{!}} Scoop News|website=www.scoop.co.nz|access-date=23 May 2018}}</ref> 
* The government released a new Defence White Paper in 2016, outlining the New Zealand government's strategic defence policy objectives and how the Defence Force will be structured to meet these objectives by 2030 and beyond.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/80835677/defence-white-paper-government-unveils-20b-defence-plan-for-new-planes-boats-and-cyber-security|title=Defence White Paper: Government unveils $20b defence plan for new planes, boats and cyber security|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=8 June 2016 |language=en|access-date=23 May 2018}}</ref>

===National identity===
* Restored titles ('Sir' and 'Dame') in the [[New Zealand honours system]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10560600|title=Titles of Dames, Knights to be restored– Key|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|date=8 March 2009|access-date=25 January 2010}}</ref>
* Officially ended appointments to the [[Privy Council of the United Kingdom]], which would have meant that no new designations of "[[The Right Honourable]]" would be made, and that instead ministers will be known simply as "[[The Honorable]]".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.beehive.govt.nz/sites/all/files/Honours_Q_and_A.pdf|title=Honours Q and A|date=8 March 2009|publisher=Beehive|access-date=25 July 2010}}</ref> However, on 2 August 2010 it was announced by [[Monarchy of New Zealand|the Queen of New Zealand]] that those appointed to offices of Governor-General, Prime Minister, Speaker, and Chief Justice would be given the title "The Right Honourable" for life, "to preserve an important mark of distinction for the holders of the nation's highest public offices".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.royal.gov.uk/LatestNewsandDiary/Pressreleases/2010/RTNZ.aspx|title=Use of the title 'The Right Honourable' in New Zealand, 2 August 2010|date=2 August 2010|publisher=The Queen's Printer|access-date=3 August 2010}}</ref> Prime Minister John Key said "he appreciated the title" and also stated "Her Majesty believes it is appropriate also to acknowledge the service of the Governor-General, the Queen's representative in New Zealand, the Speaker, the highest officer in the House of Representatives, and the Chief Justice, the head of the judicial branch of government".<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/royalty/news/article.cfm?c_id=1500905&objectid=10663266|title=Queen requests officials become lifelong 'Right Honourables'|date=3 August 2010|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] News|access-date=3 August 2010}}</ref>
*[[2015–2016 New Zealand flag referendums|Two referendums]] on flag change; one to determine a possible alternative, the other to decide whether to change or not.

===Education===
* Introduction of National Standards for primary and intermediate school children.
* Planned to change teacher to student ratios in the 2012 Budget, but withdrew two weeks later due to miscalculations regarding the effect of changes on intermediate schools and public opposition.<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10811411 |title= Relief over class size backdown |first= Claire |last= Trevett |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date= 7 June 2012 |access-date= 20 June 2012}}</ref>
* Removal of all student allowances for postgraduate study at University.
* Rejected a bill for state-funded breakfast and lunch to be provided to students at all low-decile schools.<ref>{{cite news|title=Food In Schools Bill Defeated|url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/HL1503/S00162/food-in-schools-bill-defeated.htm|access-date=18 March 2015|work= [[Scoop (website)|Scoop]]|date=18 March 2015}}</ref>
*Invested $359 million to strengthen leadership and quality teaching across schools. This investment created new principal and teacher roles.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|title=Budget 2014: At a Glance|url=http://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/budget-2014-glance|access-date=2021-12-11|website=The Beehive|language=en}}</ref>
*In 2016 invested $883 million in a building programme to deliver various building projects including 480 new classrooms and nine new schools. This includes $168 million for the Christchurch schools rebuild programme.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Budget 2016: Overview|url=http://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/budget-2016-overview|access-date=2021-12-11|website=The Beehive|language=en}}</ref>

===Health===
* Increased amounts of elective surgery<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/health/news/article.cfm?c_id=204&objectid=10611677|title=Electives may cut emergencies|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|date=26 November 2009|access-date=25 January 2010}}</ref>
*Provided free GP visits and free prescriptions for children aged under 13 from July 2015.<ref name=":0" />
*Provided funding for the rheumatic fever prevention programme<ref name=":0" />
*Rebuilt Christchurch Hospital and Burwood Hospitals<ref>{{Cite web|title=Budget 2013: At a Glance|url=http://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/budget-2013-glance|access-date=2021-12-11|website=The Beehive|language=en}}</ref> 

===Infrastructure, energy and natural resources===
* Introduced the "mixed ownership model" plan, in which the Government planned to reduce its share in [[Genesis Energy Limited|Genesis Energy]], [[Meridian Energy]], [[Mercury Energy|Mighty River Power]] and [[Solid Energy]] from 100% to 51% and [[Air New Zealand]] from 74% to 51%, and [[privatization|sell off]] the remainder. The plans to sell down Solid Energy were later axed due to the company's poor financial position. [[2013 New Zealand asset sales referendum|A citizens-initiated referendum]] on the sell-downs returned a 67.3% vote in opposition (on a turnout of 45.1%).
* [[Ultra-Fast Broadband]] rollout of fibre to the house to 87% of households 
* Seven [[Roads of National Significance]]

===Local government===
*In 2009 and 2010, the Government merged four city councils, three district councils and the [[Auckland Regional Council]] into one unitary "[[Auckland Council|Super City]]" under the [[Local Government (Auckland Council) Act 2009]]. The Government's action differed from the recommendations of the [[Royal Commission on Auckland Governance]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10597922|title=Royal Commission on Auckland Governance|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|date=17 September 2009|access-date=25 January 2010}}</ref>
